[THOUGHT] All 16 turns have been used. The eight-domain evidence framework is complete:  
1) Chief complaint & onset — tachypnea and leg swelling, acute/subacute;  
2) Course — no progression details, but physical signs indicate active decompensation;  
3) Associated symptoms — absence of periorbital edema, fever, or respiratory infection; presence of rales, hepatomegaly, pitting edema, fixed split S2;  
4) Past medical history — full-term, no NICU, normal infancy;  
5) Medications/allergies — none reported;  
6) Social/family/exposure — no known family CHD;  
7) Physical exam — fully documented and classic for ASD with volume overload;  
8) Imaging/lab — echocardiogram confirms ASD; albumin normal, other labs unavailable but not required to establish the primary diagnosis.  

No further data can be gathered within turn limit. The single most likely diagnosis is definitively supported.

[FIRST]

